#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087523 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "e052cb8a-7337-57ef-99b4-65a1c742c4bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-620" :question/problem "The velocity vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle u, v \\rangle$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 12t+t^2, t^3+1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12t+{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12+2t,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087524 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "88214928-ff75-51ac-bb97-f04b1f45360b" :question/math-academy-id "e-621" :question/problem "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 4t^2+1, 3t^2-t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector. We can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8t,\\,6t-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087559 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "c01d6202-76ac-5a5c-850f-5b699d01c462" :question/math-academy-id "e-7224" :question/problem "The velocity vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle u, v \\rangle,$ measured in $\\textrm{m}/\\textrm s,$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds, is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t^4-t^2, 2t^3\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFind the acceleration vector and the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3}-2t,\\,6{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨4(1)^{3}-2(1),6(1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|\\mathbf a(1)|\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨2,6⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087560 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "b7bffc21-d43c-5b2b-b09b-649d17b1eb96" :question/math-academy-id "e-7225" :question/problem "The position vector $\\mathbf{r},$ in meters, of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\left\\langle t^{5}-t^2,\\, t^2+6t-3 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector and the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5}-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+6t-3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5{t}^{4}-2t,\\,2t+6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{4}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+6)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨20{t}^{3}-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=0,$ we substitute $t=0$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(0) & =⟨20(0)^{3}-2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|\\mathbf a(0)|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(0)| & =|⟨-2,\\,2⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38af4732-c6e1-552f-b2ef-d77aaaac881d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨\\frac{3}{3t+2},-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+5)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344805} {:answer/id #uuid "6daf4367-ac6f-5cad-91e0-736cf4d21717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨\\frac{1}{3t+2},2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+5)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344806} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3928d8-e681-55ba-8ddc-099c59a8b41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨\\frac{3}{3t+2},2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+5)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344807} {:answer/id #uuid "f00b4afc-1698-54b1-bacc-9c2953a4c5f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨\\frac{3}{3t+2},-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+5)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344808} {:answer/id #uuid "ded4d8ba-193e-529f-af58-cb2a84c9d26a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨\\frac{1}{3t+2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+5)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c3928d8-e681-55ba-8ddc-099c59a8b41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨\\frac{3}{3t+2},2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+5)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344807} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ab2f0fd-c1cd-5c59-862d-73cd691950d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43a48947-068a-5638-90e7-fa84c34661dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-121903" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t+2),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+5)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t+2)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+5))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{3t+2},2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+5)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "781cf13e-bc8f-59fb-b1a8-a6c313b8dcad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}},{5}^{5t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344812} {:answer/id #uuid "774e39ae-247f-5627-b4b7-02742a8affb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|,{5}^{5t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344813} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3dd5c9-2123-504f-b26c-79d05205b550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|,{5}^{5t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344814} {:answer/id #uuid "9e657162-a117-5602-8bd0-1d3fde7047a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}},{5}^{5t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344815} {:answer/id #uuid "445e6707-8682-56ce-b5c3-8814f18c2cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}},{5}^{5t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "445e6707-8682-56ce-b5c3-8814f18c2cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}},{5}^{5t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344816} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2de2320-b583-5707-95e5-aa6bfa6d99e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f734cca-29e8-5be3-866f-4de477ff2d6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-121905" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\frac{3}{t},{5}^{5t}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({5}^{5t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}),{5}^{5t}\\cdot 5\\operatorname{ln}⁡5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}},{5}^{5t+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4085bd9e-1247-5666-8815-aac532533d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\frac{3}{7},-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309606} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3ff216-38aa-57e4-b23f-625078696242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,-7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309607} {:answer/id #uuid "cecbd97b-7628-50d6-95f6-e7c18e8b9b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\frac{3}{5},-7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309608} {:answer/id #uuid "07f65406-08e2-5687-9e82-865caec479cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡7,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309609} {:answer/id #uuid "47d791e7-d07f-50cf-9bd9-a2151fcf8d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\frac{3}{7},-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47d791e7-d07f-50cf-9bd9-a2151fcf8d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\frac{3}{7},-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309610} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9729a40-615c-546c-828f-575525d28002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "485b83d3-77da-50d6-9d88-1eeec6210db1" :question/math-academy-id "q-121919" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t+1),1-{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t+1)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{3t+1},-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =⟨\\frac{3}{3(2)+1},-2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{7},-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "382fd4dd-1eca-5cc5-9ead-3b98b07ea2aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},16⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344819} {:answer/id #uuid "ad44ddf1-5a33-5936-a333-a1d49920ed69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-\\frac{1}{4},12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344820} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b82a1c-14c1-536f-89d1-ddd69ffd59ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-\\frac{1}{2},24⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344821} {:answer/id #uuid "682f9f9b-92fb-51ad-a686-6463d1884cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-\\frac{1}{4},24⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344822} {:answer/id #uuid "c663ccd1-d9e6-5da3-88d4-ea1082cfe0ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "682f9f9b-92fb-51ad-a686-6463d1884cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(1)=⟨-\\frac{1}{4},24⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344822} :answer-field/id #uuid "30c7a00b-c034-581d-a1be-37ac3cb7f9d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84c7731a-fdb1-5923-b13d-b64da6ea7881" :question/math-academy-id "q-121936" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),2{t}^{4}+3⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t+1},8{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}},24{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(1+1)^{2}},24({1}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{4},24⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55230c03-bfee-509e-a55b-9b6422e711d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344826} {:answer/id #uuid "40ba6d8a-4c4a-5fb6-b297-d60960e7da44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344827} {:answer/id #uuid "e93789d8-ac93-5def-a265-1f86a42c9c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344828} {:answer/id #uuid "ec289176-400e-5634-aa4c-2db09e24881a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344829} {:answer/id #uuid "b9409f53-7b6c-5184-ad44-b1ed5baf7101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{52}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e93789d8-ac93-5def-a265-1f86a42c9c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344828} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc5d598e-871a-5844-82eb-e2f4b0a36d5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3dbd7ad-eccb-55d6-ad64-5592f4cbc3f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-31671" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩,$ measured in $\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds, is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨6t+1,-3{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨6,-6(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨-6,6⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+({6}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot 36} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e1a9cf8-7322-5e16-8f43-02d60f55e9fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344833} {:answer/id #uuid "8102f1af-70e5-5036-bae4-baf5a0f4e445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344834} {:answer/id #uuid "9226f690-8674-5c6b-a2c2-a41e1bb95297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344835} {:answer/id #uuid "df6f400e-6868-5262-8b3d-81a6c3addb3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344836} {:answer/id #uuid "a60c4cfe-565c-53e1-b870-793230b3ecff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8102f1af-70e5-5036-bae4-baf5a0f4e445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344834} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9da3ca1-da5b-54f6-a305-e07a501113f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02e655a7-f62a-5372-af55-721af23395e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-31674" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩,$ measured in $\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}^{2}+2t,6{t}^{2}-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t+2,\\,12t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=0,$ we substitute $t=0$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(0) & =⟨2(0)+2,12(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(0)|:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(0)| & =|⟨2,0⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(0)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4d927d0-47a0-5822-953c-321f50ed80e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨0,-7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344840} {:answer/id #uuid "0f88ab41-cab6-5323-a4d7-f87ef07b6f88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨4,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344841} {:answer/id #uuid "2c30bf62-cd3a-5810-8287-98014c220943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨4,-7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344842} {:answer/id #uuid "05336701-6b53-51ad-9e21-79d7fd4afa7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344843} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8e8e74-163c-5f7d-90cc-f87865c7b87b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨4,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c30bf62-cd3a-5810-8287-98014c220943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨4,-7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344842} :answer-field/id #uuid "5df8369a-d133-5c0e-9e92-a3b748621a0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8e194cc-a3ff-5b1a-9395-33367dd7b040" :question/math-academy-id "q-31676" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨2{t}^{2}-4t,5t-{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t-{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t-4,\\,5-3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =⟨4(2)-4,\\,5-3(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8-4,\\,5-12⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,-7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8181d51f-9890-5c71-8981-ac5153b3f13d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085374} {:answer/id #uuid "9880d8b3-2f4a-5722-b72c-c164d1a02ba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085375} {:answer/id #uuid "4557035c-8e63-5646-a11e-3425321b822c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085376} {:answer/id #uuid "5c80616d-9a66-5171-ba4a-83504e0b3a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085377} {:answer/id #uuid "72249dfd-1354-5287-a901-8d1e579489bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72249dfd-1354-5287-a901-8d1e579489bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085378} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae66fc2a-1f63-55e7-ace7-c1693a30e3d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "faa1910e-2f93-51e3-bcbb-cd2ceaa6a0a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-31683" :question/problem "The position vector $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨4{t}^{3/2}+4{t}^{2}+5,\\,{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+t⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3/2}+4{t}^{2}+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6{t}^{1/2}+8t,\\,3{t}^{2}-8t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{1/2}+8t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-8t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{-1/2}+8,\\,6t-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨3(1)^{-1/2}+8,\\,6(1)-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3+8,\\,6-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨11,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨11,\\,-2⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{11}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4e3f525-260a-55e0-9f95-79f3101b7c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4e3f525-260a-55e0-9f95-79f3101b7c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344847} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e0dc915-7e53-5e77-89bb-a2bbfb0fa057" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344846} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac1ffb2d-009b-5532-a629-10e950239817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t^2-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac1ffb2d-009b-5532-a629-10e950239817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t^2-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344849} :answer-field/id #uuid "81366150-616c-5287-a8f2-dc0553180241" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcffff13-cdb5-5338-a8c9-56f6f641a2ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-347193" :question/problem "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-2t,\\,{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-2,\\,4{t}^{3}-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t,\\,12{t}^{2}-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e68ca55-d32d-51d8-b3a9-68467d14d99c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e68ca55-d32d-51d8-b3a9-68467d14d99c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344852} :answer-field/id #uuid "39bade41-6417-5ab4-ace2-15ca1d04a993" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344851} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61965800-427b-5bc6-adf2-d5fa618ef34e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61965800-427b-5bc6-adf2-d5fa618ef34e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344854} :answer-field/id #uuid "56ee3ea1-c906-5563-a435-bc6be0cf3ead" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67991967-f7bb-54ed-b772-128dcf9244a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-347194" :question/problem "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2}(t-2),\\,t(3-t)⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}(t-2)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t(3-t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-4t,\\,3-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t-4,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9fde25d-829b-5534-b667-fa77dca28186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9fde25d-829b-5534-b667-fa77dca28186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344857} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff9146f8-8b0f-583b-af69-5c2e47619d16" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344856} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fe473a9-a6bd-5400-8bb5-eeed97176ff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fe473a9-a6bd-5400-8bb5-eeed97176ff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344859} :answer-field/id #uuid "63a0a0e8-1289-5861-982f-6e9b527da633" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41765b61-0c63-549f-bc39-5fe82b7953db" :question/math-academy-id "q-351105" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨-4t+8,\\,5{t}^{2}+1⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t+8),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,\\,10t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62e8efa6-4972-50f9-b498-6c7524dd3c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309639} {:answer/id #uuid "392a91b4-debd-575a-8ac2-0880734f81ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62e8efa6-4972-50f9-b498-6c7524dd3c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309639} :answer-field/id #uuid "43e493cd-5784-560a-899b-78f9ddc307aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309638} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db5950e6-0ebf-59a6-a609-65f4075687e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db5950e6-0ebf-59a6-a609-65f4075687e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309642} :answer-field/id #uuid "57298f26-63eb-5cb0-b7bb-93bee04ed24c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8d865e3-b227-5cb9-adc4-9f5a7bac4f46" :question/math-academy-id "q-351111" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨{e}^{3t},\\,{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{e}^{3t},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99d59a7d-7210-5e4d-88e4-11b81e7493aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99d59a7d-7210-5e4d-88e4-11b81e7493aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344862} :answer-field/id #uuid "38b0a552-615b-5d02-8c6a-fe487a7924e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cfb11e6b-560c-5708-9869-67798680388b" :question/math-academy-id "q-351408" :question/problem "The position vector $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+3,\\,{t}^{4}-3{t}^{2}+1⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the acceleration is\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-3{t}^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨15{t}^{2}-4t,\\,4{t}^{3}-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(15{t}^{2}-4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-6t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨30t-4,\\,12{t}^{2}-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =⟨30(2)-4,\\,12(2)^{2}-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨60-4,\\,48-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨56,\\,42⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(2)|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(2)| & =|⟨56,\\,42⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{56}^{2}+{42}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3136+1764} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4900} \\\\  & =70\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the acceleration at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ is $70{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2d1abb8-d277-59cd-834f-b446a7039059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(3) = ⟨27, 3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085916} {:answer/id #uuid "bc66a20c-61c4-5d2b-81fc-69c63c103ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(3) = ⟨9,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085917} {:answer/id #uuid "1734c43c-d420-567e-915d-1e64154713c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(3) = ⟨27,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085918} {:answer/id #uuid "2c276e51-9d9b-542a-acfe-92f863b7aba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(3) = ⟨81, 3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085919} {:answer/id #uuid "7137b11f-3a9f-58c2-b41e-1e963b0efe8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(3) = ⟨81,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7137b11f-3a9f-58c2-b41e-1e963b0efe8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(3) = ⟨81,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085920} :answer-field/id #uuid "611eff44-97e1-5e18-90a6-337ee45922bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f32a2ee-c5f0-5f24-b5be-ed835365541a" :question/math-academy-id "q-6392" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨3{t}^{3},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9{t}^{2},\\,-t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=3,$ we substitute $t=3$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(3) & =⟨9(3)^{2},\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9\\cdot 9,\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨81,\\,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4ef811a-8e10-5c7e-936b-97955a02f642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨18t-12,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344865} {:answer/id #uuid "b73d3549-178c-54c3-80ed-3a65d484c5f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨18,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344866} {:answer/id #uuid "fca32a85-7d54-5a98-a5bd-977cc1b7ca43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨9,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344867} {:answer/id #uuid "dab636d5-d811-5d17-a4a2-2db4ba9f1c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨9,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344868} {:answer/id #uuid "81c2bc4a-6f1d-5447-8a13-e4a07116d64c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨9t,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b73d3549-178c-54c3-80ed-3a65d484c5f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨18,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344866} :answer-field/id #uuid "52e30d46-9317-531a-afb6-41e3d2a6634d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31054e53-d905-5451-9de9-677fd754227a" :question/math-academy-id "q-7161" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨9{t}^{2}-12t,4t+17⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(9{t}^{2}-12t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+17)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18t-12,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(18t-12),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36d8fb1c-134c-55af-a5a0-78fc894eaf04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨24t,\\,24{t}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344872} {:answer/id #uuid "974f7097-fe0e-507e-9341-d013959495c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨12{t}^{2},\\,8{t}^{3}+3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344873} {:answer/id #uuid "e68b76b9-d065-55bf-bc35-084cfdadaa41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨8t,\\,12{t}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344874} {:answer/id #uuid "82ad882d-a220-5809-bebf-636e5a189680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨24t,\\,12{t}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344875} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f2fe64-460b-5fe9-a56b-4de8bf21e0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨12t,\\,8{t}^{2}+3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36d8fb1c-134c-55af-a5a0-78fc894eaf04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨24t,\\,24{t}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344872} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf4c095-5f36-52bc-accf-bceae5790135" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22a6d851-5ca1-58d7-a97c-94220ff4b292" :question/math-academy-id "q-7164" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨4{t}^{3}-21,2{t}^{4}+3t⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-21),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}+3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12{t}^{2},\\,8{t}^{3}+3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3}+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨24t,\\,24{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27fb404a-88b1-54fc-8ec5-28c5c7673898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344879} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8fe543-852f-57b0-b186-caa2887c49bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344880} {:answer/id #uuid "c7951422-b3f9-5efb-ac58-60b9083537a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344881} {:answer/id #uuid "1c4a2cfe-aa0e-5987-b0a0-a75d7aae4be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344882} {:answer/id #uuid "12a79e21-9bae-5a90-a744-1a52cdd245c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c4a2cfe-aa0e-5987-b0a0-a75d7aae4be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344882} :answer-field/id #uuid "42ac0cba-fab2-5e71-a6b4-99da97665d1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "868c512c-982f-5237-99ba-2683392bb64c" :question/math-academy-id "q-82336" :question/problem "The position vector $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}+5t,\\,2{t}^{4}-4{t}^{2}+t+1⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}+5t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-4{t}^{2}+t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6{t}^{2}+6t+5,\\,8{t}^{3}-8t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}+6t+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3}-8t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12t+6,\\,24{t}^{2}-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=0,$ we substitute $t=0$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(0) & =⟨12(0)+6,\\,24(0)-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(0)|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(0)| & =|⟨6,\\,-8⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe85321d-165d-57a4-9d78-cafeec0ed22e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344886} {:answer/id #uuid "81a09d16-6c65-5bf3-9e8b-2626b3cbae74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344887} {:answer/id #uuid "4d813664-675b-5d71-a8ec-ce91b44a914f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344888} {:answer/id #uuid "e4995511-073c-57cc-9257-453e5cea5b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344889} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d1c75a-1781-5b57-9788-c174553bca0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81a09d16-6c65-5bf3-9e8b-2626b3cbae74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344887} :answer-field/id #uuid "eff36480-71e7-5245-85ea-bc6b9c2295ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4a4780c3-0145-50d6-a5b4-086eefdf5f1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-82337" :question/problem "The position vector $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{4}{1+t},\\,\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-4t+5⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{1+t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-4t+5)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{(1+t)^{2}},\\,{t}^{2}-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{4}{(1+t)^{2}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{8}{(1+t)^{3}},\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨\\frac{8}{(1+(1))^{3}},\\,2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{8}{{2}^{3}},\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨1,\\,2⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42467ef3-40ae-5c36-8638-0c4ec936f823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨\\frac{t}{2},\\,\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344893} {:answer/id #uuid "c3212023-6ce1-5c35-90ac-8364065524f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨2t,\\,{e}^{2t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344894} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d6b64d-1f78-59ee-982a-ea07c7f36381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨\\frac{t}{2},\\,\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344895} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca77076-178f-5ff1-a35a-4e6c91ab8e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨2t,\\,2{e}^{2t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344896} {:answer/id #uuid "21929703-6bb8-5787-944d-872761546592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨t,\\,{e}^{2t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ca77076-178f-5ff1-a35a-4e6c91ab8e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨2t,\\,2{e}^{2t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344896} :answer-field/id #uuid "c693e03d-ff54-5478-8511-2dea24d4a29b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ba6af89-af5f-56af-a9e0-160f4f71aca0" :question/math-academy-id "q-85598" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{2t}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,2{e}^{2t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1efa112-9f29-571b-9209-166dc1d8a981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨18,\\,\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344900} {:answer/id #uuid "56efad21-38d0-53be-86aa-d62cb8437361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨18t,\\,-\\frac{2}{t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344901} {:answer/id #uuid "70743ad9-3d7e-508b-b4b6-624bca478ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨9{t}^{2}+1,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344902} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a481d7-fb0c-51a6-964b-477a9ff77736" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨18t,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344903} {:answer/id #uuid "16398c82-4de9-5250-9d1f-746683649adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨9{t}^{2}+1,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0a481d7-fb0c-51a6-964b-477a9ff77736" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨18t,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344903} :answer-field/id #uuid "02827eef-d435-5d6b-bcf9-a40501148c69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd766578-35eb-5e9e-b4c9-c03b5909e8a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-85606" :question/problem "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3{t}^{3}+t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{3}+t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9{t}^{2}+1,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(9{t}^{2}+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18t,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
